	"nodes":[
		{"id":"989d3ef0fbcab818","type":"file","file":"garden/attachments/Pasted image 20261122154233.png","x":680,"y":60,"width":400,"height":324},
		{"id":"3a1b451b906e6fc1","type":"file","file":"garden/attachments/Pasted image 20261122154250.png","x":640,"y":580,"width":400,"height":324},
		{"id":"1bf191bf0f6d6624","type":"file","file":"garden/attachments/Pasted image 20261122154219.png","x":-140,"y":-40,"width":400,"height":322},
		{"id":"d1c1832bd1ae42ce","type":"text","text":"**\n\n- Synopsis: A heavy, organic gravity wall built with regional dark charcoal and iron-stained quarry stone. It steps down naturally with the sidewalk slope, creating deep horizontal shadow lines that blend natively into Pacific Northwest landscaping.\n    \n- Contractor Cost: $7,500 – $10,500\n    \n\n- Drivers: Stone weight (~4 tons of split basalt at $450–$550/ton), flatbed crane delivery, and intensive manual mason labor to shape, level, and interlock every irregular course without mortar.\n    \n\n- Longevity: 100+ Years (Indefinite)\n    \n\n- Natural stone cannot rot, rust, or degrade. A dry-stack system allows water to seep freely through joints, completely eliminating hydrostatic pressure blowout.\n    \n\n- Key Considerations:\n    \n\n- Tolerances: Basalt faces are irregular. While the base sits 6 inches back, protruding rock faces will vary, making your gravel planting trough look more rustic and uneven.\n    \n- Leaf Cleanup: Fallen basswood leaves settle into rock crevices and need to be blown out regularly to prevent them from breaking down into compost between the stones.\n    \n\n**","x":80,"y":340,"width":503,"height":635},
		{"id":"6604815da9211357","type":"text","text":"**\n\n- Synopsis: A razor-thin, modern vertical profile with clean 90-degree corners. Over its first two seasons, it oxidizes into a rich, matte amber-rust patina that contrasts sharply against dark house siding and pale gravel.\n    \n- Contractor Cost: $6,800 – $9,200\n    \n\n- Drivers: Heavy structural plate (14-gauge lawn edging cannot hold this hill; it requires true 3/16\" plate or engineered panels), custom corner bending/welding, soil tiebacks (deadmen), and post anchors set into concrete.\n    \n\n- Longevity: 50 – 70+ Years\n    \n\n- The dense exterior oxide layer seals the steel against progressive rust. In Portland's wet climate, it weathers quickly, but 3/16\" thickness takes decades to suffer structural loss.\n    \n\n- Key Considerations:\n    \n\n- Footprint Efficiency: At just 3/16\" thick, it consumes virtually zero bed depth, preserving the maximum possible width for your upper planting terrace and keeping the 6-inch sidewalk/driveway buffer completely open.\n    \n- Rust Runoff: As it cures during the first 12–18 months of rain, runoff water carries rust particulate. Your gravel buffer is crucial here to catch the drip line and prevent permanent orange stains on your sidewalk concrete.\n    \n- Leaf Cleanup: Basswood leaves blow cleanly off the smooth steel top edge without catching.\n    \n\n**","x":1148,"y":-89,"width":672,"height":589},
		{"id":"5754e2b620d00e2e","type":"text","text":"**\n\n- Synopsis: Stacked heavy Douglas fir or hem-fir timbers finished in deep carbon-black exterior oil. It features visible saw marks, natural knotting, and checking, offering a grounded, modern-craftsman look that echoes horizontal lap siding.\n    \n- Contractor Cost: $3,500 – $5,200\n    \n\n- Drivers: Lowest material outlay (~$500–$750) and fast mechanical assembly. Timbers are rapidly cut, stacked, lagged with structural TimberLOK screws, and pinned to the subgrade with rebar stakes.\n    \n\n- Longevity: 15 – 25 Years\n    \n\n- Even with modern AWPA UC4A ground-contact pressure treatment and non-woven geotextile behind the wall, heavy exposure to damp Portland soil and persistent winter moisture will eventually cause checking, warping, and fungal decay.\n    \n\n- Key Considerations:\n    \n\n- Budget Preservation: This option costs roughly half of basalt or structural steel, leaving thousands of dollars free for other priorities.\n    \n- Maintenance: The black exterior stain or timber oil will need a wash and reapplication every 3–4 years to maintain its deep color against UV and rain wash.\n    \n- Wall Thickness: A 4×6 (3.5\" bed depth) or 6×6 (5.5\" bed depth) takes up noticeable room along the terrace perimeter compared to steel.\n    \n\n**","x":1220,"y":654,"width":945,"height":500},
		{"id":"961bb094e44310ce","x":440,"y":1038,"width":530,"height":116,"type":"text","text":"Google Doc Link: https://docs.google.com/document/d/1d_O4cL4bF552Zbx7Z9BXPCY-K3-fnlSHaiOjesnGCE4/edit?usp=sharing"}
